  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 304 AM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nSaturday through Wednesday...\n\nMoving into the weekend, a very blocky upper level pattern is \nforecast over the eastern CONUS with a large ridge axis extending \nfrom the Gulf northward into the Great Lakes.  A closed upper level \nlow will be found across New England with an early fall Nor'easter \nbringing rain and winds to much of central and northern New England. \nLocally, we'll be under the influence of the upper ridge for \nSaturday and into Sunday.  Daytime highs on Saturday and Sunday will \ntop out in the in the 71-76 range with overnight lows cooling into \nthe upper 40s in the east with lower-mid 50s in the west. \n\nMoving into next week, the upper pattern will start to shift a bit \nas the closed low over New England lifts northeastward into SE \nCanada.  Out across the western US, a fairly strong trough axis will \nmove into the four corners region.  Model solution divergence really \nstarts to show up by Tue/Wed as the GFS solutions maintain a more \nprogressive flow pattern with the upper trough pushing into the Ohio \nValley by mid-week.  On the other hand the Canadian and Euro \nsolutions appear to want to close off the trough axis into an upper \nlow and then spin it slowly eastward.  The GFS solution would argue \nfor shower/storm chances to arrive by Wednesday, while the \nEuro/Canadian solutions would result in drier conditions across the \nregion.  For now, will continue to use the multi-model consensus \nhere which will keep the forecast dry for Mon/Tue and introduce some \nchance PoPs in for Wednesday.\n\nTemperatures will continue to moderate through the period with highs \non Monday warming into the mid-upper 70s with some lower 80s over \nsouthern Kentucky.  Depending on the eventual evolution of the upper \nlevel pattern, a warm shot of air will arrive on Tuesday with \ntemperatures warming into the low-mid 80s in across southern IN and \nnorthern KY with upper 80s across southern KY. Similar readings are \nexpected on Wednesday.",
